1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,988
You find a flight for 170 euros.

2
00:00:02,988 --> 00:00:05,888
A day later, the same seat costs 260.

3
00:00:05,888 --> 00:00:09,150
Or you book, and the next morning it's cheaper.

4
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:13,307
CapFare locks the price of your flight, wherever you book.

5
00:00:13,307 --> 00:00:14,650
It runs on NEAR.

6
00:00:15,450 --> 00:00:19,177
The CapFare extension recognises the flight on any booking site.

7
00:00:19,177 --> 00:00:21,041
Here, on our demo site,

8
00:00:21,041 --> 00:00:23,650
it offers to lock a maximum price.

9
00:00:24,450 --> 00:00:26,345
No wallet, no seed phrase.

10
00:00:26,345 --> 00:00:28,997
A passkey creates a readable NEAR account,

11
00:00:28,997 --> 00:00:31,650
and the relayer pays every gas fee.

12
00:00:32,450 --> 00:00:34,617
Premiums come from prepaid credit.

13
00:00:34,617 --> 00:00:37,650
The iDEAL top-up is simulated on testnet.

14
00:00:38,450 --> 00:00:42,859
The premium comes from a public formula on the contract.

15
00:00:42,859 --> 00:00:45,505
Buying locks the full maximum payout

16
00:00:45,505 --> 00:00:48,150
in the vault before you pay.

17
00:00:48,950 --> 00:00:53,266
You choose the deductible and how long the lock lasts,

18
00:00:53,266 --> 00:00:57,150
three to thirty days. A higher deductible costs less.

19
00:00:57,950 --> 00:01:01,088
Prices here come from a scripted demo source.

20
00:01:01,088 --> 00:01:03,442
When the price passes your cap,

21
00:01:03,442 --> 00:01:06,188
you get an alert in the app,

22
00:01:06,188 --> 00:01:08,150
by push or by email.

23
00:01:08,950 --> 00:01:12,056
Book anywhere, then claim with the original email.

24
00:01:12,056 --> 00:01:15,550
Bookings can often be cancelled free for a day,

25
00:01:15,550 --> 00:01:19,821
so the payout waits for a live check of the booking.

26
00:01:19,821 --> 00:01:22,150
Then it's paid from the vault.

27
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:27,144
Bookings are checked by a verifier built for a hardware enclave.

28
00:01:27,144 --> 00:01:30,575
Only the flight, fare and price reach the chain.

29
00:01:30,575 --> 00:01:32,100
Edited or forwarded emails,

30
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:35,150
reused bookings and someone else's booking are refused.

31
00:01:35,950 --> 00:01:39,197
Just booked? The Drop Guarantee pays

32
00:01:39,197 --> 00:01:45,150
the difference if the same flight gets cheaper during its window.

33
00:01:45,950 --> 00:01:48,304
Not sure which flight or day?

34
00:01:48,304 --> 00:01:52,227
Route cover pays against the cheapest fare on the route,

35
00:01:52,227 --> 00:01:56,150
up to three days either side, and from nearby airports.

36
00:01:56,950 --> 00:01:59,995
One policy covers up to nine travellers.

37
00:01:59,995 --> 00:02:03,040
A return trip is bought as one,

38
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:05,650
and one booking claims both flights.

39
00:02:06,450 --> 00:02:10,571
Refundable tickets get their final check just before departure.

40
00:02:10,571 --> 00:02:15,150
And a Price Cap can be renewed without a gap.

41
00:02:15,950 --> 00:02:17,867
Payouts arrive as test USDC.

42
00:02:17,867 --> 00:02:20,167
Withdraw them, send them to a

43
00:02:20,167 --> 00:02:22,467
bank account through a simulated ramp,

44
00:02:22,467 --> 00:02:25,150
or quote another asset with NEAR Intents.

45
00:02:25,950 --> 00:02:29,893
Liquidity providers fund two separate vaults, one per product.

46
00:02:29,893 --> 00:02:32,521
Deposits and withdrawals settle per epoch,

47
00:02:32,521 --> 00:02:35,150
and every policy is fully collateralised.

48
00:02:35,950 --> 00:02:39,714
Everything is public: parameters, collateral, prices and service keys.

49
00:02:39,714 --> 00:02:43,059
On testnet the services still use development keys,

50
00:02:43,059 --> 00:02:45,150
and the page says so.

51
00:02:45,950 --> 00:02:48,645
Paste a link from any booking site,

52
00:02:48,645 --> 00:02:50,955
or watch a price for free

53
00:02:50,955 --> 00:02:53,650
and get an alert when it moves.

54
00:02:54,450 --> 00:02:57,291
Why NEAR? Users never touch gas.

55
00:02:57,291 --> 00:03:01,079
They sign meta-transactions and our relayer submits them.

56
00:03:01,079 --> 00:03:04,868
Accounts are readable names, created by the relayer.

57
00:03:04,868 --> 00:03:07,235
Premiums, payouts and deposits move

58
00:03:07,235 --> 00:03:09,603
as NEP-141 tokens with transfer-and-call.

59
00:03:09,603 --> 00:03:14,338
Every step that moves money is an async cross-contract call,

60
00:03:14,338 --> 00:03:18,126
with a callback that rolls back on failure.

61
00:03:18,126 --> 00:03:20,494
Price snapshots and booking attestations

62
00:03:20,494 --> 00:03:23,335
are verified on-chain with ed25519 signatures.

63
00:03:23,335 --> 00:03:26,650
And everything is on the public explorer.

64
00:03:27,450 --> 00:03:29,577
I built CapFare because a flight

65
00:03:29,577 --> 00:03:31,705
price is the one thing on

66
00:03:31,705 --> 00:03:33,832
a trip you can't plan for.

67
00:03:33,832 --> 00:03:36,668
It's a testnet prototype: live contracts, demo prices,

68
00:03:36,668 --> 00:03:39,150
and services not in an enclave yet.

69
00:03:39,950 --> 00:03:45,150
CapFare. Built at the NEAR Hackathon.
